WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.542
Welcome to Module 8 of the
Privileged Access learning path:

2
00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:06.925
evidence and drift for the ISSO.

3
00:00:06.925 --> 00:00:08.975
It's the last module of the path.

4
00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:14.481
It serves the project plan's task
A3.6 in Phase 3, A4.3 in Phase 4,

5
00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:19.313
and Gate 4, with Chapter 08
of the developer workbook.

6
00:00:19.313 --> 00:00:22.304
In it you run the privileged
reads on a schedule,

7
00:00:22.304 --> 00:00:27.214
say what changed since the last run
without putting anyone's name in the issue

8
00:00:27.214 --> 00:00:31.817
tracker, show every PIM activation in
a window with its approval, and hand

9
00:00:31.817 --> 00:00:36.574
the ISSO a package and a period summary
they can check with sha256sum alone.

10
00:00:36.574 --> 00:00:41.111
This lab uses two kits: the
privileged-access folder in PowerShell,

11
00:00:41.111 --> 00:00:44.575
and the drift pipeline in
a POSIX shell with Python.

12
00:00:44.575 --> 00:00:47.840
It's illustrative:
tested against fixtures.

13
00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:52.715
Why this module?

14
00:00:52.715 --> 00:00:58.415
The plan's last gate, Gate 4, asks for
evidence for every elevation in the period.

15
00:00:58.415 --> 00:01:01.315
Two facts from Microsoft
Learn shape how you get it.

16
00:01:01.315 --> 00:01:06.549
Microsoft Learn's page on Entra data
retention says audit logs are kept for thirty

17
00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:08.859
days with Microsoft Entra I D P1 or P2,

18
00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:11.553
so you have to read them
before they're gone.

19
00:01:11.553 --> 00:01:16.178
And Microsoft Learn's page on PIM audit
history shows that PIM can log one

20
00:01:16.178 --> 00:01:21.189
activation under several correlation I
D's, while its request I D stays the same.

21
00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:23.915
And one thing carries over from Module 2:

22
00:01:23.915 --> 00:01:28.540
every run repeats the emergency
access check beside the inventory.

23
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.951
The comparison treats the
emergency access rules,

24
00:01:31.951 --> 00:01:36.417
the Global Administrator count and
root-scope elevation as Tier 0,

25
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:41.290
whatever the row says, and any new or
changed Tier 0 finding is an incident.

26
00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:47.060
That's the program's drift rule: a drift
on a Tier 0 object is a security incident.

27
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.689
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:56.372
which takes about sixty-five
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:56.372 --> 00:02:01.133
You'll explain why the run reads both the
tenant's state and the PIM audit events,

30
00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:03.697
and why the audit windows
must leave no gap.

31
00:02:03.697 --> 00:02:07.848
You'll read the audit findings,
audit rules oh one to oh four.

32
00:02:07.848 --> 00:02:10.148
You'll explain how the comparison works,

33
00:02:10.148 --> 00:02:12.797
by fingerprint, and what
counts as an incident.

34
00:02:12.797 --> 00:02:15.798
You'll explain why issue
drafts carry no names.

35
00:02:15.798 --> 00:02:18.370
And you'll package, chain and verify runs,

36
00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.098
and assemble a period
for the Gate 4 review.

37
00:02:21.098 --> 00:02:27.472
Before you start, finish Modules 1 and 2:
the run repeats their two reads unchanged.

38
00:02:27.472 --> 00:02:30.777
And skim Chapter 09 of the
first developer workbook,

39
00:02:30.777 --> 00:02:33.697
which the comparison and
the package build on.

40
00:02:33.697 --> 00:02:40.092
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or later
with Pester 5, and for the POSIX steps,

41
00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:45.640
Python 3.10 or later with pytest
and PyYAML, and sha256sum.

42
00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.928
Unit 1: what the evidence has to show.

43
00:02:51.928 --> 00:02:53.615
The run has three parts.

44
00:02:53.615 --> 00:02:57.877
The inventory and the emergency
check describe the tenant's state:

45
00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.427
who holds what, right now.

46
00:02:59.427 --> 00:03:02.955
The PIM audit read describes
the events in a window:

47
00:03:02.955 --> 00:03:06.566
every request, approval,
activation and assignment.

48
00:03:06.566 --> 00:03:09.552
And a chain of windows with
no gap between them is

49
00:03:09.552 --> 00:03:12.540
what every elevation in
the period means in code.

50
00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.852
That's this workbook's design guidance.

51
00:03:14.852 --> 00:03:19.627
The audit read refuses a window that starts
more than thirty days before the run.

52
00:03:19.627 --> 00:03:22.917
With thirty days' retention,
older events can't be read,

53
00:03:22.917 --> 00:03:27.103
and a window reaching further back
would look complete when it isn't.

54
00:03:27.103 --> 00:03:31.889
Microsoft Learn's page on PIM audit
history makes the same point from the other

55
00:03:31.889 --> 00:03:34.393
side: its data covers
the past thirty days,

56
00:03:34.393 --> 00:03:37.265
so route the logs elsewhere
to keep them longer.

57
00:03:37.265 --> 00:03:41.620
And the read keeps each
event as I D's, not names.

58
00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.707
The audit findings,
rules oh one to oh four.

59
00:03:48.707 --> 00:03:51.646
Audit oh one is a role
assigned outside PIM.

60
00:03:51.646 --> 00:03:56.088
Microsoft Learn's security operations
guide for PIM says to monitor

61
00:03:56.088 --> 00:04:00.220
roles assigned outside PIM, and it's
a finding for Tier 0 and Tier 1.

62
00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:05.022
Audit oh two is a Tier 0 or Tier 1
activation that completed with no approval

63
00:04:05.022 --> 00:04:09.670
record in the window, because the
program's tiers require approval there.

64
00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:14.718
Audit oh three is an activation approved
by its own requester; the plan's Gate 3

65
00:04:14.718 --> 00:04:18.426
asks for requester and approver
to be distinct for Tier 0,

66
00:04:18.426 --> 00:04:20.082
and that's the kit's design.

67
00:04:20.082 --> 00:04:23.283
And audit oh four is a role
setting changed in PIM.

68
00:04:23.283 --> 00:04:26.719
Microsoft Learn's guide says
to always alert on PIM setting

69
00:04:26.719 --> 00:04:30.857
changes for Privileged Role
Administrator and Global Administrator.

70
00:04:30.857 --> 00:04:33.513
The change is a finding
until it's reconciled

71
00:04:33.513 --> 00:04:36.740
with a reviewed change to
the files from Module 4.

72
00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.890
Unit 2: compare, package and the period.

73
00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:48.702
The comparison matches findings between
two runs by fingerprint, not by name.

74
00:04:48.702 --> 00:04:52.952
Each finding is new, changed,
resolved or unchanged.

75
00:04:52.952 --> 00:04:56.953
A task that didn't read the
tenant this run is unverified,

76
00:04:56.953 --> 00:05:01.302
and its findings stay open, never
resolved: silence isn't a fix.

77
00:05:01.302 --> 00:05:07.066
And a new or changed Tier 0 finding, or
drift on a Tier 0 object, is an incident.

78
00:05:07.066 --> 00:05:09.665
Then the issue drafts carry no names.

79
00:05:09.665 --> 00:05:12.092
Their titles come from a fixed catalog,

80
00:05:12.092 --> 00:05:15.353
and their bodies carry object
I D's and fingerprints.

81
00:05:15.353 --> 00:05:17.407
The names stay in the drift report,

82
00:05:17.407 --> 00:05:22.028
which goes to the access-controlled
evidence store, not to the issue tracker.

83
00:05:22.028 --> 00:05:23.751
That's this workbook's design,

84
00:05:23.751 --> 00:05:27.490
so the tracker never becomes
a list of privileged people.

85
00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.890
The package and the chain.

86
00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:39.240
Each run becomes a package, with an
evidence manifest and a SHA256SUMS file.

87
00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.000
Each package records the previous
package's manifest hash,

88
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.765
so the packages form a chain.

89
00:05:44.765 --> 00:05:49.340
A replaced package, or a missing day,
shows up as a break in that chain.

90
00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:53.401
And the reviewer doesn't need
the code to check it: sha256sum,

91
00:05:53.401 --> 00:05:58.953
checking the sums file, is enough, and the
kit's verify command checks the manifest.

92
00:05:58.953 --> 00:06:05.264
Then what the ISSO receives: a period, with
its own manifest and a period summary.

93
00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:08.799
The summary speaks to the Gate
4 criteria the code can measure,

94
00:06:08.799 --> 00:06:11.860
and says plainly which
ones it leaves to people.

95
00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:16.335
Try it.

96
00:06:16.335 --> 00:06:21.410
Steps one to four run in PowerShell
7, in the privileged-access folder.

97
00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:24.750
Step one runs Microsoft
Learn's queries from its PIM

98
00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.935
audit history page over
the fixture, as published.

99
00:06:27.935 --> 00:06:32.010
Eight pass; the twenty-four not
run are the chapter's other tests.

100
00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:36.439
The third test is the one to notice:
two of the thirteen rows the first query

101
00:06:36.439 --> 00:06:39.271
returns are role assignments
made outside PIM,

102
00:06:39.271 --> 00:06:41.522
which a reader of the query could miss.

103
00:06:41.522 --> 00:06:44.322
Step two lists the
permissions the run needs.

104
00:06:44.322 --> 00:06:47.739
Nine: Audit Log dot Read
dot All for the events,

105
00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:51.710
and the read permissions
Modules 1 and 2 already use.

106
00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.510
None ends in ReadWrite.

107
00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:56.540
The output on the slide is wrapped to fit.

108
00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.765
Step three runs two days
against the fictional tenant.

109
00:07:03.765 --> 00:07:08.394
First, one variable holds the run's
settings: the cloud, the tenant I D,

110
00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:12.855
the output folder, the audit
fixture, the emergency access files,

111
00:07:12.855 --> 00:07:17.652
the exceptions, the Azure scopes,
and the control-plane subscription.

112
00:07:17.652 --> 00:07:22.315
Then run day one, as of six in the
morning on the twenty-ninth of October.

113
00:07:22.315 --> 00:07:27.038
The inventory, the emergency check
and the PIM audit read all report OK,

114
00:07:27.038 --> 00:07:30.540
and the audit window is the
twenty-four hours before.

115
00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:34.765
Then day two, against the second
day's tenant, as of the thirtieth.

116
00:07:34.765 --> 00:07:39.177
Notice the second window starts
exactly where the first ended: no gap.

117
00:07:39.177 --> 00:07:43.740
Each run's folder has a run file that
lists each task's manifest hash.

118
00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:48.174
On your machine, the Written to line
may print the folder's full path;

119
00:07:48.174 --> 00:07:51.280
the path's verification log records that.

120
00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:58.142
Step four reads the second
day's audit findings.

121
00:07:58.142 --> 00:07:59.931
Three, all Tier 0.

122
00:07:59.931 --> 00:08:04.443
Audit oh one: a Privileged Role
Administrator assigned outside PIM.

123
00:08:04.443 --> 00:08:09.093
Audit oh two: a Global Administrator
activation with no approval.

124
00:08:09.093 --> 00:08:13.243
And audit oh four: a Global
Administrator role setting change.

125
00:08:13.243 --> 00:08:17.430
Open the events file and you'll
see object I D's, no names.

126
00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:21.618
Step five moves to a POSIX shell,
in the drift pipeline folder.

127
00:08:21.618 --> 00:08:26.124
Two variables name the two days' runs:
the repository's copies of the ones

128
00:08:26.124 --> 00:08:30.555
you made in step three, and a Pester
test checks them against a fresh run.

129
00:08:30.555 --> 00:08:34.080
Compare day one with nothing
before it: it's a baseline,

130
00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.005
with twenty-six new findings,
and the compare exits with two.

131
00:08:38.005 --> 00:08:39.455
Then package day one.

132
00:08:39.455 --> 00:08:42.969
The package prints its
evidence manifest hash,

133
00:08:42.969 --> 00:08:46.300
starting e one oh d eight five four three.

134
00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:53.112
Step six compares the
next day with the first.

135
00:08:53.112 --> 00:08:54.637
It's not a baseline now.

136
00:08:54.637 --> 00:08:58.651
One assignment added, one
removed, three audit findings,

137
00:08:58.651 --> 00:09:03.450
two new findings, six resolved,
twenty unchanged, and six incidents.

138
00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:06.774
The summary's incident
table lists all six,

139
00:09:06.774 --> 00:09:10.762
each Tier 0, each with its
object I D and fingerprint.

140
00:09:10.762 --> 00:09:13.605
A Privileged Role
Administrator assignment added,

141
00:09:13.605 --> 00:09:16.249
and a Global Administrator
assignment removed.

142
00:09:16.249 --> 00:09:20.013
A new I N V oh one finding for
the person who got that role.

143
00:09:20.013 --> 00:09:22.574
And the three audit
findings from step four.

144
00:09:22.574 --> 00:09:26.094
The summary ends by saying the
names are in the drift report,

145
00:09:26.094 --> 00:09:29.325
which goes to the evidence
store, not the issue tracker.

146
00:09:29.325 --> 00:09:31.100
The compare exits with two.

147
00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.987
And among the six resolved findings
are Module 2's E A oh seven and two

148
00:09:35.987 --> 00:09:41.230
E A oh eight findings for E A oh two,
which is now in the exclusion group.

149
00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:48.905
Step seven drafts the findings
for the issue tracker:

150
00:09:48.905 --> 00:09:52.205
seven to open, nine to
close, none to comment on.

151
00:09:52.205 --> 00:09:55.817
Then search the drafts for
the fictional domain, contoso.

152
00:09:55.817 --> 00:10:01.592
Zero matches, and grep exits with one: no
user principal name reaches the drafts.

153
00:10:01.592 --> 00:10:05.381
Step eight packages the second
day, chained to the first:

154
00:10:05.381 --> 00:10:09.580
you pass day one's evidence
manifest as the previous manifest.

155
00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:14.149
The new package's hash starts
seven six three d four a one f.

156
00:10:14.149 --> 00:10:17.455
Verify it: fifteen files,
and the same hash.

157
00:10:17.455 --> 00:10:20.099
Then check it the way a reviewer would,

158
00:10:20.099 --> 00:10:23.255
with sha256sum alone,
checking the sums file.

159
00:10:23.255 --> 00:10:25.780
It prints nothing and exits with zero.

160
00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:27.554
In the evidence manifest,

161
00:10:27.554 --> 00:10:31.720
the previous package's hash is
day one's: that's the chain.

162
00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:38.487
Step nine assembles the
period for the ISSO,

163
00:10:38.487 --> 00:10:42.007
from the twenty-eighth to
the thirtieth of October.

164
00:10:42.007 --> 00:10:45.925
Two packages, no chain breaks,
no audit coverage gaps,

165
00:10:45.925 --> 00:10:48.658
six incidents, and complete is false.

166
00:10:48.658 --> 00:10:54.323
The summary counts three activations,
two Tier 0 and one Tier 1; four requests,

167
00:10:54.323 --> 00:11:00.258
two approved and one denied; and one Tier
0 or Tier 1 activation without approval.

168
00:11:00.258 --> 00:11:01.870
Then the Gate 4 table.

169
00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:03.921
Residual standing privilege:

170
00:11:03.921 --> 00:11:08.432
one on the exceptions list with a
reason, eight outside it, so not met.

171
00:11:08.432 --> 00:11:11.493
Evidence for every
elevation in the period:

172
00:11:11.493 --> 00:11:14.808
not met, because one
activation had no approval.

173
00:11:14.808 --> 00:11:19.187
Whether the exception register is
current, and whether the access-related

174
00:11:19.187 --> 00:11:23.708
group policy settings are retired, are
left to people: not decided by this code.

175
00:11:23.708 --> 00:11:25.433
The period exits with two.

176
00:11:25.433 --> 00:11:28.545
Object I D's only; the
packages hold the names,

177
00:11:28.545 --> 00:11:31.930
under the evidence store's access control.

178
00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:37.668
Step ten breaks it on purpose.

179
00:11:37.668 --> 00:11:41.485
Append a word to the drift
summary inside day two's package,

180
00:11:41.485 --> 00:11:44.905
and verify it: the file doesn't
match its hash, an error.

181
00:11:44.905 --> 00:11:47.752
Compare the days the
wrong way round: refused,

182
00:11:47.752 --> 00:11:51.368
because the current run isn't
later than the previous one.

183
00:11:51.368 --> 00:11:53.430
Each refusal exits with one.

184
00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:57.430
And the sample workflow that would run
all this on a schedule lints clean.

185
00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.218
Step eleven runs the tests in both shells.

186
00:12:00.218 --> 00:12:03.622
In PowerShell, the evidence
tests, thirty-two pass,

187
00:12:03.622 --> 00:12:06.529
then the whole suite,
three hundred thirty.

188
00:12:06.529 --> 00:12:11.505
In the POSIX shell, pytest in the drift
pipeline: a hundred forty-two pass.

189
00:12:11.505 --> 00:12:16.030
The path's verification log notes that
this suite needs the whole repository,

190
00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:18.080
not just the drift pipeline folder.

191
00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.630
Then delete the output
folder in both places,

192
00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:23.950
because they hold the
fixture's privileged holders.

193
00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:33.155
Lab 2 runs in a worldwide or G C C test
tenant with Microsoft Entra I D P2 or I D

194
00:12:33.155 --> 00:12:39.325
Governance and a test Azure subscription,
never production, with fictional people.

195
00:12:39.325 --> 00:12:41.063
The run only reads.

196
00:12:41.063 --> 00:12:44.443
It uses the emergency
access files from Module

197
00:12:44.443 --> 00:12:47.737
2's Lab 2 and the exceptions
file from Module 4's.

198
00:12:47.737 --> 00:12:53.362
Register a reader app, make one test
activation, and run twice, before and after it.

199
00:12:53.362 --> 00:12:55.595
Check the fields the fixture assumed,

200
00:12:55.595 --> 00:12:59.412
and cross-check against PIM's
resource audit in the admin center.

201
00:12:59.412 --> 00:13:03.124
Then compare, package and
verify the two live runs.

202
00:13:03.124 --> 00:13:06.711
The reader app needs the nine
read permissions from step two,

203
00:13:06.711 --> 00:13:10.800
with admin consent, and Reader on
the test subscription, nothing else.

204
00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:15.595
The back-out ends the test activation
and eligibility, removes the permissions,

205
00:13:15.595 --> 00:13:20.172
the Reader assignment and the app, and
deletes the test runs and the packages,

206
00:13:20.172 --> 00:13:22.788
which hold the tenant's
privileged holders.

207
00:13:22.788 --> 00:13:25.290
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

208
00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:30.427
The federal requirement.

209
00:13:30.427 --> 00:13:36.069
The runs, the packages and the period
are the evidence for the plan's tasks

210
00:13:36.069 --> 00:13:41.428
A3.6 and A4.3, and for Gate 4's evidence
for every elevation in the period.

211
00:13:41.428 --> 00:13:47.124
From NIST special publication eight
hundred fifty-three they support C A seven,

212
00:13:47.124 --> 00:13:50.577
continuous monitoring; A
C six, enhancement nine,

213
00:13:50.577 --> 00:13:54.892
logging the use of privileged
functions; A U six, audit review;

214
00:13:54.892 --> 00:13:59.553
A U nine, protection of audit
information; and A U eleven, retention.

215
00:13:59.553 --> 00:14:03.152
Drift as an incident adds
S I four and I R four.

216
00:14:03.152 --> 00:14:08.213
And from CISA's B O D twenty-five
oh-one and its ScubaGear baseline:

217
00:14:08.213 --> 00:14:12.524
M S A A D seven point five,
roles assigned only through PIM,

218
00:14:12.524 --> 00:14:17.491
and seven point six, approval to
activate Global Administrator.

219
00:14:17.491 --> 00:14:21.528
The evidence an ISSO keeps
for Gate 4: each run's folder;

220
00:14:21.528 --> 00:14:26.575
each package's manifest and sums,
in the evidence store; the period's

221
00:14:26.575 --> 00:14:32.890
manifest and summary; and the incident
record each Tier 0 fingerprint matches.

222
00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:38.615
Check yourself with two questions.

223
00:14:38.615 --> 00:14:41.288
First: why does the audit
read refuse a window

224
00:14:41.288 --> 00:14:44.177
that starts more than
thirty days before the run?

225
00:14:44.177 --> 00:14:49.699
The answer is b: Microsoft Entra audit logs
are kept for thirty days with P1 or P2,

226
00:14:49.699 --> 00:14:54.965
so older events can't be read, and the
window would look complete when it isn't.

227
00:14:54.965 --> 00:14:58.315
Second: what does the hash
chain between packages show?

228
00:14:58.315 --> 00:15:02.603
The answer is b: a replaced package
or a missing day, as a break,

229
00:15:02.603 --> 00:15:07.503
because each package records the
previous package's manifest hash.

230
